    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "I added the sheet's terms, due-by and overdue, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` now pass.\n\n**Stored data and server**\n- A new migration, `V20__sheet_terms.sql`, adds a `terms` column to the sheet. It defaults to 14 days, so existing sheets get 14.\n- The settings endpoint now reads and saves `terms` as a whole number of days. Saving it writes `SETTING_CHANGED sheet=<id> by=<user> key=terms value=<days>` to the audit file.\n\n**Front end**\n- **Settings:** a new `setting-terms` input and a `setting-terms-current` display. Like the other settings, terms are only saved and audited when you change them.\n- **Each claim:** `claim-due-by` shows the claim's date plus the terms. `claim-overdue` shows yes when the claim is OPEN and its due-by is before the sheet's today.\n- **Each person and the sheet:** `person-overdue-count`/`person-overdue-total` and `rollup-overdue-count`/`rollup-overdue-total`. These cover only overdue claims that already count in the totals, so a held person's open claims are left out. A claim with no rate (n/a) adds nothing to the overdue total.\n\n**One fix outside the request.** Scenario 2 failed on an older gap. Clicking Edit on a draft claim dated in a closed period opened the form. The server then refused the save, but the test was told the edit had worked because the amount already showed the value it typed. The Edit button now refuses such a claim straight away and shows `claim-error`, the same way it already refuses non-draft claims. This is in `person-claims.ts`.\n\nChoices I made that the request didn't specify:\n- **Valid terms:** 0 to 9999 whole days.\n- **Sheet overdue total:** it adds up every person's overdue total, including people whose own due-total shows n/a.\n- **No audit lines for overdue:** a terms change doesn't add ROLLUP or TOTALS lines, because those lines don't include the overdue figures.",
